1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
There was a time when buying
something meant you actually owned it.

2
00:00:04,085 --> 00:00:06,385
If you bought an album,
you owned the record.

3
00:00:07,185 --> 00:00:09,265
If you bought a film, you owned the tape.

4
00:00:09,765 --> 00:00:12,085
If you bought a game, you owned the disc.

5
00:00:12,845 --> 00:00:14,605
And if you bought a
piece of software, nobody

6
00:00:14,835 --> 00:00:17,365
expected you to pay for
it again next month.

7
00:00:18,411 --> 00:00:23,671
Today, we pay every month. For music.
For films. For software. For storage.

8
00:00:23,751 --> 00:00:24,951
For games.

9
00:00:25,038 --> 00:00:27,278
And more and more things that
used to be simply bought...

10
00:00:27,508 --> 00:00:29,978
are quietly turning into services.

11
00:00:31,473 --> 00:00:33,993
For a company, the arithmetic is simple.

12
00:00:34,773 --> 00:00:36,903
A customer who buys a program once, for a

13
00:00:37,133 --> 00:00:39,932
hundred dollars, is
worth a hundred dollars.

14
00:00:40,232 --> 00:00:44,592
A customer who pays ten dollars a month
is worth a hundred and twenty a year.

15
00:00:45,152 --> 00:00:46,272
If they stay for ten years...

16
00:00:46,502 --> 00:00:48,393
twelve hundred.

17
00:00:49,293 --> 00:00:51,893
So what do you actually
buy when you subscribe?

18
00:00:52,813 --> 00:00:54,913
And could you still live
without subscriptions?

19
00:00:56,191 --> 00:00:59,551
The easy answer is that
companies pushed all this on us.

20
00:00:59,951 --> 00:01:01,791
That's not quite what happened.

21
00:01:04,051 --> 00:01:09,211
To see why, it helps to go back to
something very simple. A cassette tape.

22
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:14,150
If you had the cassette, you had
the music. You could copy it.

23
00:01:14,590 --> 00:01:17,830
You could put it on a shelf and
forget about it for twenty years.

24
00:01:18,563 --> 00:01:20,843
Nobody could switch it off remotely.

25
00:01:21,403 --> 00:01:24,303
Nobody could remove the
album from your region.

26
00:01:24,663 --> 00:01:27,223
Nobody could change the terms of service.

27
00:01:27,601 --> 00:01:30,101
And nobody could send
you an email saying...

28
00:01:30,221 --> 00:01:32,101
Your subscription has expired.

29
00:01:32,881 --> 00:01:38,041
The same was true for books, CDs, DVDs,
and software. You bought something.

30
00:01:38,121 --> 00:01:40,881
You took it home. And it was yours.

31
00:01:41,885 --> 00:01:45,365
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

32
00:01:45,985 --> 00:01:47,605
It really helps the channel.

33
00:01:49,975 --> 00:01:52,095
Then computers became common.

34
00:01:52,695 --> 00:01:55,655
In theory, that should have
made ownership even easier.

35
00:01:56,323 --> 00:01:59,863
Instead of hundreds of CDs,
thousands of songs as files.

36
00:02:00,903 --> 00:02:04,143
Instead of shelves of DVDs,
films on a hard drive.

37
00:02:04,843 --> 00:02:07,623
And files are easy to
copy, back up, and move.

38
00:02:08,263 --> 00:02:10,003
There was just one problem.

39
00:02:10,543 --> 00:02:12,763
Most people didn't want to deal with them.

40
00:02:13,353 --> 00:02:15,313
Files. Folders. Drives. Backups.

41
00:02:15,543 --> 00:02:17,053
And the eternal question...

42
00:02:17,273 --> 00:02:18,893
Where did I save that thing?

43
00:02:19,508 --> 00:02:22,688
So Microsoft and Apple
started hiding the complexity.

44
00:02:23,608 --> 00:02:26,268
You didn't need to know where
your photos were stored.

45
00:02:26,968 --> 00:02:28,508
You just opened Photos.

46
00:02:29,379 --> 00:02:31,319
It was a genuine improvement.

47
00:02:32,039 --> 00:02:35,559
Millions of people could use a
computer without knowing how it worked.

48
00:02:36,279 --> 00:02:38,119
And that was exactly the point.

49
00:02:40,369 --> 00:02:44,029
Then came the cloud, and it
solved an even bigger problem.

50
00:02:44,649 --> 00:02:47,409
Your files no longer had
to live on your computer.

51
00:02:48,232 --> 00:02:50,272
Your photos could be everywhere.

52
00:02:50,712 --> 00:02:52,512
Your documents could be everywhere.

53
00:02:53,378 --> 00:02:54,778
Buy a new phone, log in...

54
00:02:54,858 --> 00:02:59,338
and everything is already there.
No copying. No restoring backups.

55
00:02:59,418 --> 00:03:01,278
No digging through old hard drives.

56
00:03:02,481 --> 00:03:04,201
But something subtle happened.

57
00:03:04,661 --> 00:03:06,261
We moved from "I have the file"...

58
00:03:06,341 --> 00:03:08,481
to "I have access to the file".

59
00:03:09,307 --> 00:03:11,647
When you bought a CD, you bought a thing.

60
00:03:12,227 --> 00:03:14,947
When you subscribe to
Spotify, you buy access.

61
00:03:15,677 --> 00:03:18,137
When you bought a DVD, you bought a copy.

62
00:03:18,857 --> 00:03:22,197
When you subscribe to Netflix,
you buy access to a catalogue.

63
00:03:22,601 --> 00:03:27,761
And catalogues change. A film can
disappear. The price can go up.

64
00:03:27,841 --> 00:03:29,181
The terms can change.

65
00:03:29,675 --> 00:03:32,555
And if you stop paying...
the access disappears.

66
00:03:32,635 --> 00:03:34,235
The money doesn't come back.

67
00:03:34,891 --> 00:03:36,531
That's perfectly fair.

68
00:03:36,931 --> 00:03:40,651
You paid for the time you had
access. But it isn't ownership.

69
00:03:42,855 --> 00:03:44,975
At first, it all seemed reasonable.

70
00:03:45,495 --> 00:03:49,915
Don't want to buy a record
collection? Fine. Pay for Spotify.

71
00:03:50,806 --> 00:03:54,566
Don't want a shelf full of
DVDs? Fine. Pay for Netflix.

72
00:03:54,646 --> 00:03:58,326
Don't want to install and
maintain your own software? Fine.

73
00:03:58,906 --> 00:04:00,366
Pay for a cloud service.

74
00:04:00,859 --> 00:04:03,479
And none of this means
subscriptions are a trick.

75
00:04:04,039 --> 00:04:05,699
They solve real problems.

76
00:04:06,482 --> 00:04:09,222
If you want to hear almost
any song you can think of,

77
00:04:09,302 --> 00:04:14,422
you don't want to spend ten years building
a collection. You want to press Play.

78
00:04:15,113 --> 00:04:16,813
If you want to watch a film tonight, you

79
00:04:17,043 --> 00:04:21,273
don't want to order a disc and wait
for it. You want to press Play.

80
00:04:21,995 --> 00:04:23,575
Convenience is valuable.

81
00:04:23,655 --> 00:04:26,495
And people are perfectly
willing to pay for it.

82
00:04:28,835 --> 00:04:31,135
Software shows the trade most clearly.

83
00:04:32,075 --> 00:04:34,515
Imagine buying a program in 1998.

84
00:04:34,995 --> 00:04:38,175
You installed it from a CD,
and used it for ten years.

85
00:04:38,832 --> 00:04:40,412
The company might disappear.

86
00:04:41,132 --> 00:04:42,572
The website might disappear.

87
00:04:42,652 --> 00:04:46,192
But the program was still on your
hard drive... and it still ran.

88
00:04:46,823 --> 00:04:51,283
Today, many programs work differently.
You don't buy the program.

89
00:04:51,943 --> 00:04:53,723
You subscribe to the service.

90
00:04:54,383 --> 00:04:56,643
And if you stop paying,
it may stop working.

91
00:04:57,379 --> 00:04:59,059
Remember the hundred dollars?

92
00:04:59,139 --> 00:05:02,082
This is how it turns into twelve hundred.

93
00:05:03,981 --> 00:05:05,521
So could you go back?

94
00:05:05,861 --> 00:05:08,001
Could you actually live
without subscriptions?

95
00:05:08,986 --> 00:05:11,906
Surprisingly... yes. At
least for most things.

96
00:05:12,645 --> 00:05:14,485
Linux, with thousands of free programs.

97
00:05:14,715 --> 00:05:18,325
LibreOffice instead of Microsoft 365.

98
00:05:19,245 --> 00:05:20,745
Music bought as files.

99
00:05:20,905 --> 00:05:23,335
Films on physical discs.

100
00:05:23,525 --> 00:05:26,665
Games that don't need a
permanent online service.

101
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:31,600
Your own photos on your own
disks. Your own home server.

102
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:35,280
Even some AI models, running
on your own computer.

103
00:05:35,715 --> 00:05:37,895
And once the hardware is paid for...

104
00:05:37,995 --> 00:05:40,335
there doesn't have to
be another monthly bill.

105
00:05:41,373 --> 00:05:45,213
But there is a price. Not
necessarily money. Effort.

106
00:05:45,823 --> 00:05:49,923
If you own your data, you have to
look after it. You make backups.

107
00:05:50,003 --> 00:05:51,543
You replace failing disks.

108
00:05:52,183 --> 00:05:54,403
You move old files to new formats.

109
00:05:55,143 --> 00:05:56,923
You remember where things are.

110
00:05:57,488 --> 00:05:59,668
If you own your films, you store them.

111
00:06:00,328 --> 00:06:02,688
If you own your music, you organise it.

112
00:06:03,148 --> 00:06:06,308
If you own your software, you
install and update it yourself.

113
00:06:07,145 --> 00:06:09,085
The cloud takes all of that away.

114
00:06:09,865 --> 00:06:12,525
That's what you're really
paying for. Not storage.

115
00:06:12,605 --> 00:06:14,525
Someone else to worry about it.

116
00:06:16,125 --> 00:06:17,705
Imagine two options.

117
00:06:18,465 --> 00:06:20,285
Option one: you own everything.

118
00:06:21,005 --> 00:06:23,665
A home server, three
backups, local copies.

119
00:06:24,285 --> 00:06:25,605
You organise your files.

120
00:06:25,685 --> 00:06:27,265
You maintain your hardware.

121
00:06:27,965 --> 00:06:31,125
And now and then, a whole
Saturday spent fixing something.

122
00:06:31,696 --> 00:06:34,316
Option two: twenty dollars
a month in subscriptions.

123
00:06:34,596 --> 00:06:36,416
Everything syncs on its own.

124
00:06:37,116 --> 00:06:39,136
Your photos appear on your phone.

125
00:06:39,516 --> 00:06:41,056
Your documents are everywhere.

126
00:06:41,556 --> 00:06:43,356
Your music is always there.

127
00:06:43,896 --> 00:06:45,736
Your films are one click away.

128
00:06:46,413 --> 00:06:49,633
For most people, option two
is probably more convenient.

129
00:06:50,373 --> 00:06:52,573
And that's a perfectly rational choice.

130
00:06:53,623 --> 00:06:56,203
And that's the real
reason subscriptions won.

131
00:06:56,903 --> 00:07:00,983
Companies didn't force us to stop
owning things. We gave up ownership...

132
00:07:01,213 --> 00:07:03,863
because convenience was worth it.

133
00:07:04,747 --> 00:07:07,267
At least, until it stops being worth it.

134
00:07:07,873 --> 00:07:10,993
Because there is one question
worth asking now and then.

135
00:07:11,253 --> 00:07:14,353
What happens if the service
disappears tomorrow?

136
00:07:15,053 --> 00:07:17,273
For a DVD, the answer is simple.

137
00:07:17,713 --> 00:07:19,233
You still have the DVD.

138
00:07:20,033 --> 00:07:21,853
For a book, you still have the book.

139
00:07:22,273 --> 00:07:25,133
For a file on your own disk,
you still have the file.

140
00:07:25,758 --> 00:07:27,798
For a subscription? It depends.

141
00:07:28,670 --> 00:07:30,910
Maybe you just lose a film catalogue.

142
00:07:31,730 --> 00:07:33,590
Maybe you lose years of photos.

143
00:07:34,330 --> 00:07:37,070
Maybe you lose the software
you need for your work.

144
00:07:37,487 --> 00:07:39,027
And this isn't just theoretical.

145
00:07:40,167 --> 00:07:44,767
In 2024, Ubisoft shut down the servers
for a racing game called The Crew.

146
00:07:45,247 --> 00:07:46,667
People had bought it.

147
00:07:46,747 --> 00:07:49,267
But it needed those servers to run...

148
00:07:49,347 --> 00:07:51,067
so it simply stopped running.

149
00:07:51,733 --> 00:07:55,653
Days later, Ubisoft began removing
the game from players' accounts.

150
00:07:56,353 --> 00:07:57,993
No offline version.

151
00:07:58,373 --> 00:07:59,913
No general refund.

152
00:08:00,993 --> 00:08:03,413
And the service doesn't
even have to disappear.

153
00:08:03,953 --> 00:08:05,553
All of this works beautifully...

154
00:08:05,873 --> 00:08:07,673
as long as your internet does.

155
00:08:08,145 --> 00:08:09,785
A storm. A cut cable.

156
00:08:10,205 --> 00:08:11,925
A few days without a signal.

157
00:08:12,225 --> 00:08:15,325
And suddenly, much of what you
pay for... is out of reach.

158
00:08:17,165 --> 00:08:22,025
The question isn't whether the service
is good or bad. It's who is in control.

159
00:08:23,031 --> 00:08:24,791
And that's worth teaching children.

160
00:08:24,871 --> 00:08:27,091
Not that the cloud is bad.

161
00:08:27,351 --> 00:08:30,431
Not that everyone should build
a server in their basement.

162
00:08:30,631 --> 00:08:32,551
But what a file is.

163
00:08:32,771 --> 00:08:35,371
Where their photos actually live.

164
00:08:35,771 --> 00:08:37,451
Who controls their account.

165
00:08:38,111 --> 00:08:41,611
And the difference between owning
something and being allowed to use it.

166
00:08:42,168 --> 00:08:43,688
Then the choice is theirs.

167
00:08:44,508 --> 00:08:46,328
Netflix, because they like it.

168
00:08:46,868 --> 00:08:50,328
Cloud storage, because running
a server isn't worth their time.

169
00:08:50,408 --> 00:08:52,528
But they'll know what they're buying.

170
00:08:53,635 --> 00:08:56,755
And that may be the strangest
part of the whole story.

171
00:08:57,415 --> 00:09:00,795
Technology gave us something our
parents could barely imagine.

172
00:09:01,456 --> 00:09:03,316
Almost unlimited storage.

173
00:09:03,636 --> 00:09:05,176
Perfect copies.

174
00:09:05,276 --> 00:09:07,136
Instant distribution.

175
00:09:07,316 --> 00:09:09,756
Access from anywhere in the world.

176
00:09:10,247 --> 00:09:14,467
And yet... in 1995, a person
might have owned hundreds of CDs.

177
00:09:15,527 --> 00:09:19,567
In 2026, they might have access
to over a hundred million songs...

178
00:09:19,887 --> 00:09:21,227
and own none of them.

179
00:09:22,116 --> 00:09:23,816
That's an incredible trade.

180
00:09:24,176 --> 00:09:25,916
And maybe it's a good one.

181
00:09:26,216 --> 00:09:28,956
Maybe convenience is
worth more than ownership.

182
00:09:29,736 --> 00:09:31,856
Maybe we don't need to own everything.

183
00:09:31,936 --> 00:09:35,376
But we should at least recognise
the trade we're making.

184
00:09:35,780 --> 00:09:38,480
Because there's a big difference
between "I have it"...

185
00:09:38,560 --> 00:09:41,460
and "I can use it, as long
as someone else lets me."

186
00:09:42,220 --> 00:09:45,420
And maybe the future doesn't
have to be one or the other.

187
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:48,140
We can have the convenience of services...

188
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:52,000
and still keep our own copy of
the things that actually matter.

189
00:09:52,686 --> 00:09:54,966
A subscription can be incredibly useful.

190
00:09:55,046 --> 00:09:59,906
But there's something strangely satisfying
about a file on your own disk...

191
00:10:00,086 --> 00:10:03,246
that nobody can take away just
because you stopped paying.

192
00:10:03,699 --> 00:10:05,339
Or books on your own shelves.

193
00:10:05,419 --> 00:10:07,719
Or a stack of vinyl records.

194
00:10:08,797 --> 00:10:11,097
Because sometimes, the most
modern thing you can do...

195
00:10:11,327 --> 00:10:13,107
is own your own stuff.
